<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to check your own citations</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list"><!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Decide the one correct version of your name, address and phone number. Write it down.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Search Google for your business name, then your phone number, then any old phone numbers.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Open each result and note where the details are wrong.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Claim and correct the listings, starting with the best-known sites.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->

<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Remove or merge duplicates where the site allows it.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item --></ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Which directories matter</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Start with the places people and search engines actually use: Google Business Profile, Bing Places, Apple Maps, Yell and Facebook. Then add directories specific to your trade or town, where a listing also brings real customers. A few dozen good, accurate listings do more than hundreds of obscure ones.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">If you do not have a public address</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Many trades work from home or travel to customers. You can hide your address on Google and list the areas you serve. On directories that insist on an address, use the same one every time, or choose directories that support service-area businesses.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:group {"className":"ifm-faq","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<div class="wp-block-group ifm-faq"><!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>How many citations do I need?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There is no fixed number. Cover the main sites accurately, then add relevant trade and local directories.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Do citations help me rank?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>They support your position in local results by confirming your details. They work alongside your Google profile, reviews and website. Nobody can promise a position.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>I have moved. What should I do?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Update your Google Business Profile first, then your website, then every listing you can find. Search for the old address and phone number to catch the ones you have forgotten.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details -->

<!-- wp:details -->
<details class="wp-block-details"><summary>Are paid directories worth it?</summary><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Occasionally, for a respected trade body. Most of the benefit comes from free listings on well-known sites.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></details>
<!-- /wp:details --></div>
